  "data": {
    "A": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle alternative: sort and deduplicate blocked coordinates, precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2n-2, and count paths to each obstacle and the destination by binomial counts minus contributions from preceding reachable obstacles. Use 64-bit modular products. Its O(n+m^2) cost requires a general size-based crossover to the existing DP, not unconditional adoption. Handle blocked endpoints, duplicates and n=1 exactly; the obstacle-free binomial formula is a general algorithmic case.",
    "B": "Develop a reusable ARM64 NEON open-span scan from 85b460b1a34648e7. Accept and return a nonzero incoming carry so it can process tile rows as well as complete rows; reset only at actual obstacles. Investigate modest unrolling and vector carry propagation, retaining scalar short spans and tails. Reduce after each bounded addition and benchmark independently: the earlier SIMD variant was slower than scalar, so vectorization is not automatically beneficial.",
    "C": "Refine the leading wavefront implementation 8ad10638231442c4. Research tile dimensions and serial crossover for four cores, balancing diagonal barriers, available tile parallelism and local-buffer locality. Investigate compact row-offset obstacle storage and avoiding repeated lower_bound searches without introducing shared cursor races. Preserve disjoint boundary ownership on each diagonal, barrier publication, partial tiles and origin initialization. Prefer this direction over the slower two-barriers-per-row scan unless measurements justify revisiting it.",
    "G": "Use 8ad10638231442c4 as the incumbent. Integrate B's carry-aware scan into C's tile-local open intervals as one isolated experiment; separately evaluate A's exact sparse-obstacle dispatch and any obstacle-storage improvements. Measure complete integrations under the unchanged harness and retain the fastest correct program, including the unchanged incumbent if combinations regress. Validate with existing correctness facilities and reason explicitly about n=1, blocked endpoints, duplicate obstacles, boundary obstacles, non-multiple tile widths, modular overflow and OpenMP races. Do not change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target or permissions; dispatch only on general input properties.",
    "analysis": "The source implements Problem D, not the other problems in the combined statement. Baseline time is 81.13 ms; the best admissible program is C's tiled wavefront at 11.90 ms, approximately 6.82x faster. Row-parallel scanning takes 19.08 ms, B's segmented NEON implementation 20.65 ms, and A's scalar spans 24.88 ms. These results favor reduced synchronization and tile locality, but changes also differ in input handling and obstacle storage, so they do not isolate causal gains. Four-core ARM64 supports complementary research in sparse combinatorial counting, SIMD span kernels and tiled parallelism. Existing correctness evidence is limited; all valid inputs must remain correct, and integrated speedups must be measured rather than assumed additive."
